sy films  that felt like they opened up the  ceiling of what a story could show and  so  kind of the synthesis of all of those  ideas came together to become the  screenplay for a boy called christmas  yeah and it shows in the screenplay but  it translates to the screen too which is  so impressive they will do both um no  problem  henry my question for you here is your  scene partner in this film a lot is a  mouse uh what are you actually talking  to and looking at and was that  challenging  um  most of the time i'm talking to a little  metal pin on my shoulder which was just  a magnet underneath my jacket and  most of the time i was talking to gil to  be honest um wow  yeah i i i do a mean mouse you know i'll  never  i'll i'll never um i'll never let steven  merchant off the hook for coming in and  stealing my best lines in this film yeah  i think  i think you missed an opportunity  casting yourself to be honest  thanks so much henry there's always a  sequel say that yeah  never know  um yeah no that that's actually really  funny to hear and  uh that's interesting too sometimes when  the director's the one you're kind of  speaking to while doing it that's really  cool um yeah now  gil is there any other christmas films  that kind of inspired you of how you  were gonna bring this to the screen  yeah i mean my favorite christmas films  um  sort of  there's a couple of them i'll mention  one is a christmas story because it's a  stone cold tonal classic like the way  that that film  messes with your expectations of family  and holiday and all of the trappings  that you take for granted and sort of  bends them inside out  it has made it timeless and  a perennial we watch it almost every  year  the other one i'll mention is is sort of  more recent in my in my life but made a  huge impact on me which is nightmare  before christmas and  when when that film came out i was in  high school i remember we all went as a  as a drama class to go see it and  it felt like a vision o